# fanin-qos1 — the shared-consumer shape: every publisher in the cluster feeds one $share
# group of about a hundred PERSISTENT consumers, QoS 1, durable cluster. The public
# scenario this compares to is EMQX's open benchmark suite (github.com/emqx/mqttbs),
# "sharedsub-50K-500-50K-50K": 50,000 publishers at 1 msg/s, QoS 1, 16-byte payloads, 500
# subscribers in one `$share/benchmark/test/#` group, which EMQX's reference figures call
# "fan-in". Here the h3 client count stands — 20,000 publishers (DEF_PUBLISHERS) — with
# 18 consumers a topic and one topic a generator: 108 consumers on six generators, 36 a
# broker on three, so prefer-local deals evenly. A consumer takes about a hundredth of its
# node's publish rate, where durable-qos1's takes a few messages a second: the PUBACK
# batches of ADR 0092 T4 are tens of acks a read here, one there. The ladder starts at
# 20,000 msg/s, the suite's 1 msg/s a publisher.
#
# Values here are defaults: anything exported in the environment wins.
QOS=${QOS:-1}
SHAPE=${SHAPE:-share}
SESSIONS=${SESSIONS:-durable}
SESSION_EXPIRY=${SESSION_EXPIRY:-30}
PAYLOAD=${PAYLOAD:-200}
# One topic per generator process: all of its publishers into one group.
TOPICS=${TOPICS:-1}
# Consumers per topic and group: a multiple of the brokers, so each holds as many.
CONSUMERS=${CONSUMERS:-18}
SHARE_GROUPS=${SHARE_GROUPS:-1}
RATE_PER_PUBLISHER=${RATE_PER_PUBLISHER:-50}
INFLIGHT=${INFLIGHT:-128}
LATENCY_GATE=${LATENCY_GATE:-ack}
# Where the ramp starts, per tier (msg/s offered across the whole cluster).
START_RATE_local=${START_RATE_local:-500}
START_RATE_n3=${START_RATE_n3:-10000}
START_RATE_n10=${START_RATE_n10:-20000}
START_RATE_h3=${START_RATE_h3:-20000}
